1. Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command (CWE-89) - High Confidence
2. Insufficient Input Validation (CWE-20) - Moderate Confidence

The primary issue lies with the lack of input validation for the username and password during registration. Although the function uses parameterization to prevent SQL injection, if an attacker supplies a username containing special characters or unexpected data types (e.g., numbers or date strings), it may cause issues in the database query and potentially lead to unexpected behavior.

The second issue is related to input validation regarding the password. Even though the password is being hashed before storing, there's no check for minimum length or complexity requirements. This could make the application susceptible to brute-force attacks if weak passwords are allowed.

It is essential to ensure proper user input validation and sanitization measures to mitigate these potential vulnerabilities.
</analysis>

