1. **Token Manipulation**: An attacker can create their own JWT by encoding data in the payload and using any secret key they choose. Since the signature is not verified, these tokens will be considered valid.
2. **Data Tampering**: Even if a valid token is used, an attacker can modify the payload of the token (e.g., change permissions or add sensitive information) since no validation is performed on it.

Both of these issues can compromise the security of your application, as they allow unauthorized access and data manipulation. The lack of signature verification also violates secure JWT usage principles.

</analysis>

