Firstly, the use of `int(input(...))` can lead to a potential Denial of Service (DoS) vulnerability due to the fact that this function does not handle non-integer inputs robustly. If a user enters a non-numeric value, the program will crash with an exception. This could potentially allow an attacker to cause the application to crash or become unresponsive.

Secondly, the use of `assert type(userInput) == int` is unlikely to prevent any potential attacks because it only checks if the input is an integer after the fact. If the input is not an integer initially (due to a malicious user), this check will still fail and raise an exception.

