<analysis>
This function seems to be secure from an initial viewpoint, but it has one potential security vulnerability related to error handling and logging. The `logging.error` statement does not handle the exception properly; instead of simply capturing any errors that occur within the try block, it captures all exceptions, including unrelated ones.

This can result in a denial-of-service situation where an adversary might pass bogus input to this function and end up flooding the logs with useless error messages. 

Additionally, since userInput is not returned anywhere else after logging the exception, it will always be None which could potentially confuse other parts of the code that rely on correct return values.

</analysis>

