1. Infinite Loop: The while loop will continue running forever until the program is terminated because there is no break statement inside the loop. This could potentially hog resources unless explicitly stopped (e.g., by a keyboard interrupt). 
2. Exceptions and Input Validation: While the function attempts to convert user input to an integer, if an exception occurs due to invalid input or other unexpected conditions such as receiving SIGINT during execution (i.e., Ctrl+C in most terminals), the code will crash without handling that scenario which is generally not a good practice.
3. Resource Leak: If this function was used inside another part of the application and user continues entering string instead of integer, then it may consume unnecessary resources, defeating the purpose of having an input conversion utility. 

However, if the primary goal of the function is to capture integer inputs from users without crashing or consuming unnecessary resources (which seems likely as this is a common use case), there are no serious security vulnerabilities in this code snippet that I can see. 

